{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbbR5Yyx2FCK7tkUeMQf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, and the self-check prints `OK`. On its 100-draw smoke fit the ELPD-LOO was -2268.9. The incumbent's full fit scores -2272.9, but a smoke fit isn't comparable to that, so this says nothing yet about whether it beats the incumbent.\n\n**Refined model:** `asymmetric_ideal_alternation_mirror`, the live model closest to the incumbent (23.8 nats behind).\n\n**One change:** I added the incumbent's run-length variety term. A sequence whose runs all have the same length (HTHTHTHT, HHTTHHTT) looks rhythmic and designed. One mixing runs of different lengths looks random. A new shared weight, `nu`, scales the number of distinct run lengths relative to the most the sequence's length allows. Everything else in the model is unchanged: each person's ideal switching rate and sensitivity, the shared over-alternation fraction `omega`, personal weights on heads/tails imbalance and the longest run, shared weights on the final run and on palindromes, and a personal guessing rate.\n\n**Why:** The critique's one flagged discrepancy is that in pairs where both sequences alternate a lot, people pick the more alternating one more often than the incumbent predicts (p = 0.01, q = 0.08, so it misses the FDR cutoff). The incumbent has the variety term but a symmetric penalty around the ideal switching rate. This model has the asymmetric penalty but no variety term. Combining them lets the fit measure how lenient people are toward over-alternation separately from the rhythm penalty that strictly alternating sequences also get.\n\n**Risks:**\n- **Convergence and the Pareto-k warning:** The smoke fit ran one chain, so convergence wasn't checked. It also flagged 7.8% of trials as having high Pareto k, which makes its LOO estimate unreliable. With only 100 draws that warning is expected; the real test is admission's full fit.\n- **Duplicate risk:** This round's two incumbent-refinement slots may well add the same asymmetry to the incumbent. That would give them the same mechanism as this model, and whichever is admitted second could be rejected as a near-duplicate. The other candidates hadn't written anything when I checked, so I couldn't rule it out.\n\nFiles are in `iter_4/candidate_5/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt` (`asymmetric_alternation_run_variety`)\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3027,"cache_read_input_tokens":84068,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3027,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3bcfc5de-9065-4bf2-b32c-f6b342324a43","uuid":"e6052441-b322-44c8-9492-d3dfccda8cf8","timestamp":"2026-10-01T13:27:12.356Z","request_id":"req_011CfbbR4NmuscHBAmK9WrZg"}
